<article class="env-lemma" id="701AD3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/701AD3">Lemma <span data-tag="701AD3">5.3.1.18</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be a filtered $\infty $-category. Then $\calC $ is weakly contractible. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $\calC $ is filtered, it is nonempty. Fix an object $C \in \calC $. Let $| \calC | $ denote the geometric realization of $\calC $ as a simplicial set. We identify $C$ with a point of the topological space $| \calC |$. By Whitehead's theorem, to show that $\calC $ is weakly contractible, it suffices to show that for every $i \geq 0$, the homotopy set $\pi _{i}( |\calC |, C)$ consists of a single point. If not, we can find a finite simplicial subset $K \subseteq \calC $ containing $C$ such that the map $f: \pi _{i}( |K|, C) \rightarrow \pi _{i}( | \calC |,C)$ has nontrivial image. But $\calC $ is filtered, so the inclusion $K \subseteq \calC $ factors through a map $K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calC $. It follows that $f$ factors through $\pi _{i}( |K^{\triangleright }|, C)$. But this homotopy set is trivial, since $K^{\triangleright }$ is weakly contractible. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>